---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const pageTitle = "Likutay Halachos - nizkay mamon 1";
const pageDescription = "Breslov teachings from Likutay Halachos: nizkay mamon 1";
---
<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <div class="page-banner">
    <h1>Likutay Halachos</h1>
    <p>Choshen Mishpat - 2 - nizkay mamon 1</p>
  </div>
<style>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=EB+Garamond:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,700;1,400;1,500&family=Cinzel:wght@400;600;700&family=Noto+Serif+Hebrew&display=swap');
  .lh-container { --ink:#1c1c2e;--parchment:#f7f3ea;--parchment2:#eee8d5;--gold:#a07830;--gold-light:#c8a050;--sapphire:#1e3a5f;--sapphire2:#2e5580;--sapphire-pale:#dde8f5;--ruby:#7a1a2e;--border:#b89860;--muted:#5a5040;--verse-color:#1e3a5f;--source-color:#888070; font-family:'EB Garamond',Georgia,serif; font-size:18px; line-height:1.85; color:var(--ink); background:var(--parchment); text-align:justify; max-width:800px; margin:0 auto; padding:40px 20px 80px; }
  .lh-container .book-title { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 50px; padding-bottom: 30px; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border); }
  .lh-container .book-title h1 { font-family: 'Cinzel', serif; font-size: 2.2em; font-weight: 700; color: var(--sapphire); letter-spacing: 0.03em; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .lh-container .section-header { text-align: center; margin: 50px 0 30px; padding: 15px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .lh-container .halacha-header { text-align: center; margin: 55px 0 20px; padding: 20px 0; border-top: 2px solid var(--gold); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--gold); }
  .lh-container .sa-excerpt { text-align: center; margin: 20px 0 35px; padding: 15px 25px; background: var(--parchment2); border-left: 3px solid var(--gold); border-right: 3px solid var(--gold); font-style: italic; }
  .lh-container .ois { font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.15em; color: var(--gold); margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 10px; display: block; }
  .lh-container p { margin-bottom: 18px; text-indent: 1.5em; }
  .lh-container p:first-of-type, .lh-container .ois + p, .lh-container .sa-excerpt + p { text-indent: 0; }
  .lh-container .heb { font-style: italic; color: var(--ruby); }
  .page-banner { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a365d 0%, #2c5282 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem 2rem; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 0; }
  .page-banner h1 { font-family: 'Cinzel', serif; font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: white; }
</style>
  <div class="lh-container">
<div class="page-wrap"><h1 class="main-title">Damages of Property</h1><h2 class="sub-title">Halacha 1</h2><div class="source-ref">Likutay Halachos, Choshen Mishpat, Part II &nbsp;|&nbsp; Hilchos Nizkay Mamon</div><div class="opening-law">The matter of the four primary categories of damage [<em>arba avos nezikin</em>] that a person is obligated to guard against causing harm &mdash; namely the ox [<em>shor</em>], the pit [<em>bor</em>], the grazing animal [<em>mav&rsquo;eh</em>], and fire [<em>hav&rsquo;er</em>].</div><div class="section-header">Section 1</div><p>According to the Torah <span class="verse-inline">&ldquo;I am Hashem your G&#8209;d&rdquo;</span> in Likutay Moharan Part&nbsp;I (&sect;4) &mdash; see the entire Torah there. The general principle: there are <span class="concept">four elements</span> [<em>arba yesodos</em>] &mdash; fire, air, water, and earth &mdash; which are the aspects of the inanimate, the vegetative, the animate, and the speaking. From these four elements all bad character traits and their offshoots are drawn, as explained in detail in <em>Mishnas Chassidim</em>: all the traits drawn from each element individually. And one who wishes to walk the holy path must break all the bad character traits and evil desires drawn from these four elements &mdash; and must recount them before the Torah scholar [<em>talmid chacham</em>], meaning verbal confession [<em>vidduy devarim</em>], and the Torah scholar will detail and clarify for him the path according to the root of his soul, etc. &mdash; and through this one is included within the Infinite [<em>Ayn Sof</em>], etc., and through this one merits the aspect of the <span class="concept">Good and the Beneficent</span> [<em>ha-Tov v&rsquo;ha-Maytiv</em>]: knowing that all that befalls him is for his good &mdash; which is the aspect of a foretaste of the World to Come, the aspect of entirely good and beneficent, as explained there at length. See there.</p><p>And this is the aspect of the <span class="concept">four primary categories of damage</span> [<em>arba avos nezikin</em>] &mdash; that a person is obligated to guard them greatly against causing harm. For the four primary categories of damage are drawn from the aspect of the evil in the four elements, from which all evil desires and bad character traits and their offshoots are drawn &mdash; and from them, heaven forfend, all sins are drawn. This is the aspect of the four primary categories of damage corresponding to the four elements mentioned above. For the sins and bad character traits drawn from the four elements are the aspect of the damagers of the world [<em>mazikay alma</em>], since all harms in the world are drawn from them. And therefore in the future, when everyone will return to Hashem in truth and the spirit of impurity will be removed from the earth and the evil in the four elements will be nullified, truly no harm will exist in the world any longer &mdash; as it is written (Yeshayah 11): <span class="verse-inline">&ldquo;They shall not harm nor destroy&hellip; for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of Hashem.&rdquo;</span></p><p>And therefore the four categories are called <em>avos</em> [primary categories / &ldquo;fathers&rdquo;] &mdash; to teach us that there are also <em>toldos</em> [derivatives / &ldquo;offspring&rdquo;] (as our Sages of blessed memory said in Bava Kama 2) &mdash; for in the bad character traits drawn from the four elements there are likewise <em>avos</em> and <em>toldos</em>, as is brought in the Torah mentioned above: <em>sadness and its derivatives&hellip; arrogance and its derivatives&hellip;</em> And therefore they are the four primary categories of damage corresponding to the four elements, as above.</p><p>And this is the aspect of the ox [<em>shor</em>], the pit [<em>bor</em>], the grazing animal [<em>mav&rsquo;eh</em>], and the fire [<em>hav&rsquo;er</em>] &mdash; for they correspond to the four elements: fire, air, water, earth, which are the aspects of inanimate, vegetative, animate, speaking &mdash; as is known. For the ox is the aspect of the animate, corresponding to the element of air. The pit is the aspect of the inanimate, corresponding to the element of earth. The <em>mav&rsquo;eh</em> &mdash; which is the tooth [<em>shen</em>] &mdash; is the aspect of evil desires drawn from the vegetative, from the element of water, as brought there; this is the aspect of the tooth, the desire for eating and drinking, which are the primary desires from which all other desires follow, as explained in the words of Rabbainu of blessed memory in the Torah <em>&ldquo;VaYasev Elokim&rdquo;</em> (&sect;62). And <em>hav&rsquo;er</em> &mdash; fire &mdash; is the aspect of the element of fire, corresponding to the aspect of the speaking [<em>medaber</em>], which is the aspect of arrogance and its derivatives, as written there. It follows that the four primary categories of damage correspond to the four elements, from which all harms in the world are drawn.</p><p>And therefore damages are assessed from the best land [<em>idis</em>] &mdash; as it is written: <span class="verse-inline">&ldquo;the best of his field and the best of his vineyard shall he pay.&rdquo;</span> For the rectification of the four primary categories of damage &mdash; which are the aspects of the four elements &mdash; is through the aspect of the World to Come [<em>alma d&rsquo;asay</em>], which is the aspect of entirely good and beneficent &mdash; for then all harms will be nullified, as above. One merits the aspect of entirely the Good and the Beneficent through rectifying the four elements by breaking all the bad character traits drawn from them. And therefore the damages of the four primary categories must be paid from the best [<em>miytav</em>] &mdash; as it is written: <span class="verse-inline">&ldquo;the best of his field and the best of his vineyard shall he pay&rdquo;</span> &mdash; in order to draw thereby the aspect of entirely the Good and the Beneficent, the aspect of the World to Come, when all harms will be nullified, as above. For the payment of damages is the aspect of the rectification of harms, and their rectification is through the best [<em>miytav</em>] precisely &mdash; in order to draw thereby the aspect of entirely the Good and the Beneficent, which is the rectification of the four elements, as above.</p></div>
  </div>
</Layout>